Given: $\lim _{x \rightarrow \infty}\left(\frac{x^{2}+x+1}{x+1}-a x-b\right)=4\begin{array}{l}

\Rightarrow \lim _{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{x^{2}+x+1-a x^{2}-a x-b x-b}{x+1}=4 \\

\Rightarrow \lim _{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{(1-a) x^{2}+(1-a-b) x+(1-b)}{x+1}=4

\end{array}

For this limit to be finite $1-a=0 \Rightarrow a=1$ then given limit reduces to

$\lim _{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{-b x+(1-b)}{x+1}=4 \Rightarrow \lim _{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{-b+\frac{(1-b)}{x}}{1+\frac{1}{x}}=4\Rightarrow-b=4$ or $b=-4, \therefore a=1, b=-4

